//******************************************************************************
//
// This file contains a Translators Brief lexicon of Extended Strongs for Greek.
//
// Once you have stripped off these head-of-file comments, this file is in JSON
// format.
//
// The file is based upon https://raw.githubusercontent.com/STEPBible/STEPBible-Data/refs/heads/master/Lexicons/TBESG%20-%20Translators%20Brief%20lexicon%20of%20Extended%20Strongs%20for%20Greek%20-%20STEPBible.org%20CC%20BY.txt,
// to which you should refer for a detailed explanation of the content.
//    
// Each record is keyed upon a dStrong value, and contains the following
// fields:
//
// - eStrong.  See below.
//
// - uStrong.  See below.
//
// - Greek: Unicode lexical form.  Based on LSJ but conforming to BADG when
//     the difference may be confusing.
//
// - Transliteration: Transliteration of the Greek into Roman characters.
//
// - Morph: See below.
//
// - Gloss: Meaning in as few words as possible (by Tyndale Scholars).
//
// - Meaning: Extended meaning.  Marked with the source from which the
//   meaning was taken:
//
//   . (AS): Abbott Smith, from https://github.com/translatable-exegetical-tools/Abbott-Smith,
//     with corrections and adapted by Tyndale Scholars.
//
//   . (ML): Middle Liddell, from Perseus.  Used where there is no entry in AS.
//
//
//
// Strongs numbers
// ===============
//
// Strongs numbers here come in three flavours -- eStrong (extended Strongs),
// dStrong (STEPBible's disambiguated Strongs), and uStrong (STEPBible's
// unified Strongs).  A lexicon or tagging system will normally use only one set,
// depending on its purpose, though it may be useful to keep both dStrong & uStrong.
//
// Each system has its own advantages and disadvantages:
//
// eStrong is the standard Extended Strongs which conforms with the subdivisions of
// words in BDB (Brown Driver Brigg's lexicon), largely followed by later lexicons.
// The work here was carried out by OS (OpenScriptures.org).
//
// dStrong has separate numbers when a given name is used for different people or
// places, and when a word has significantly different sub-meanings. See
// TinyURL.com/DisambiguatedStrongs.
//
// uStrong has a single number covering all of an individual's various names, and
// a single number for a word that has different forms or spellings or that has a
// Hebrew and Aramaic version.
//
// For example,	Strong used G2264 for all three people in the NT named Herod
// (the Great, Anitipas and Agrippa I), and a different number for 'Herodian',
// which describes Herod Anitipas:
//
// - eStrong does nott divide up any names in the NT (in contrast to the way things
//   work in the OT) eStrong was created to map Strongs to the Hebrew lexicon BDB.
//
// - dStrong has a unique number for each person: G2264G, G2264H, G2264I.  As in
//   the OT, letters start at G when there is no "a" or "b" etc in eStrongs
//   (which never happens in Greek).
//
// - uStrong has only one unique number for each individual, so a search for that
//   number finds all the places where that person is mentioned no matter how their
//   name is spelled.
//
// See further discussion at TinyURL.com/STEP-Disambiguated.
//
//
// eStrongs numbers are as follows:
//
// - G0001 - G5624: The Strongs numbers from original set made by Strong for the
//   text behind the KJV.
//
// - G3203 - G3302: Not used for some reason.
//
// - G5627 - G5799: Greek morphology codes (not used in this lexicon).
//
// - G5800 - G5893: Greek Synonyms - see http://studybible.info/strongs/G5800
//   (not used in this lexicon).
//
// - G6000 - G6090: Extra Strongs Numbers in NASB mainly for variants not known
//   to Strong.  (They remain the same for the sake of backward compatibility
//   with NASB.)
//
// - G6100 - G9979: Extra Greek from Apostolic Bible Project, mainly for LXX
//   words not found in NT (including Greek names that do not occur in the Hebrew).
//
// - G6091 - G6099, G9980 - G9999: Extra Greek for variants which are not included
//   in NASB or Apostolic Bible.
//
// - G10000 - 19999: Greek equivalent of Strongs Hebrew numbers for names and
//   transliterated words eg Abagtha (H0005) = G10005. When there is a NT
//   equivalent, this is the STEPBible lexical tag.
//
// - G20000 - G20199: Words where the Full LSJ entry refers to the LXX, but
//   which are not referred to in the AB tagging because they occur only in
//   non-Protestant canons or in MSS other than Vaticanus (used by AB).
//
//
//
//
// Morphology
// ==========
//
// The 'morph' field gives the simple gramatical value of the main word,
// represented as Language:Type-Gender-Extra.
//
// - Language: A=Aramaic, H=Hebrew, G=Greek and N=Name (this latter
//   option being not language specific).
//
// - Type is A=Adjective, Adv=Adverb, Art=Article, Cond=Conditional,
//   Conj=Conjunction, Cor=Correlative, DemP=Demonstrative Pn,
//   ImpP=Impersonal Pn, Intg=Interogative, Intj=Interjection,
//   N=Noun, Neg=Negative, Part=Particle, Prep=Preposition,
//   PerP=Personal Pn, PosP=Possessive Pn, RefP=Reflexive Pn,
//   RelP=Relative Pn, V=Verb.
//
// - Gender is F=Female, M=Male, N=Neuter, C=Common, and P or S is
//    optionally added for Plural or Singular.
//
// - 'Extra' for Names is L=Location, P=Person, LG/PG=Gentilic, T=Title
//   (ie any other capitalised nouns such as titles, months, gods, planets,
//   etc).
